Welcome to Episode 3 of Franklin, where we found the city of Franklin.

This episode will delve into early urban planning theory, and then we'll see how the best-laid plans of our founder, William Franklin, are immediately trashed. 

We'll also look at the importance of various kinds of forced or otherwise unfair labor in the early colony. 

We'll also talk a bit about distilling.

Some useful links w/r/t early urban planning:

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippodamus_of_Miletus
http://theworldisurban.com/2011/06/history-of-the-grid/
https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2016/mar/22/story-of-cities-7-philadelphia-grid-pennsylvania-william-penn-america-urban-dream


Sorry for the variable voice levels -- I'm still getting used to the new mic.


Note to copyright guys: the music is a public domain recording of Edvard Greig's "In the Hall of the Mountain King" from Musopen -- I did not steal your music. You can see for yourself here: https://musopen.org/music/777-peer-gynt-suite-no-1-op-46/

The title music is Beethoven's Symphony no. 5 in Cm, Op. 67 - IV. Allegro, performed by the Davis High School Symphony Orchestra. This is also public domain music from Musopen: https://musopen.org/music/2567-symphony-no-5-in-cm-op-67/

